 # Octree-based structures
 
-Also called: Sparse Voxel Octrees (SVO), Voxel Octrees
+Also called: Sparse Voxel Octrees (SVO), Voxel Octrees, Implicit Octrees
 
 ## Overview
-Octree-based structures represent 3D volumetric data by recursively subdividing the spatial domain into eight child nodes, storing data only in occupied regions to achieve compression proportional to sparsity. They differ from sibling methods like [Sparse Voxel DAGs](2791-sparse-voxel-dags.md) by maintaining a strict tree topology (no node sharing) and from [Compact pointer trees](844-compact-pointer-tree.md) by using a fixed branching factor of 8, which simplifies address calculation but may result in deeper trees for the same resolution [S1, unofficial][S2].
+Octree-based structures represent 3D volumetric data by recursively subdividing the spatial domain into eight child nodes per parent, storing data only in occupied regions to achieve compression proportional to sparsity [G]. They solve the memory and bandwidth bottlenecks of dense voxel grids by enabling efficient culling of empty space and adaptive resolution, distinguishing themselves from sibling methods like [Sparse Voxel DAGs](2791-sparse-voxel-dags.md) (which merge identical subtrees) and [Pointer Trees](4139-tree-based-structures-octree-pointer-tree-sparse-voxel-dag.md) (which use variable branching factors) by maintaining a strict binary-tree-of-cubes topology [G].
 
 ## Shared principles
 All structures in this family share the following mechanism:
 
-1.  **Recursive Subdivision:** The 3D domain is recursively divided into sub-volumes. In standard octrees, each node has 8 children. In pointer trees, the branching factor $N$ varies ($N^3$ children).
-2.  **Sparsity Encoding:** Empty regions are represented by null pointers or absence of children, rather than storing zero-valued voxels. This provides compression proportional to the sparsity of the data.
-3.  **Hierarchical Traversal:** Rendering or access requires traversing from the root to the leaf. This allows skipping large empty volumes (culling) and adapting resolution to view distance or detail requirements.
